n = 7, 21 edges, cosets = 2^15
cut space |C| = 64 (expect 2^6 = 64)
coset representatives: 32768

======================================================================
RESULT at n = 7
======================================================================
  labelled switching classes that FAIL (some automorphism fixes no member): 0
  EMPTY.
  So n = 7 joins n = 3 and n = 5: the failure set is empty at every odd n
  computed so far, which is STRICTLY STRONGER than klein-S338's theorem
  (that covers only n = 1 mod 4) and needs a separate argument at 3 mod 4.

  Moon's theorem supplies half of it: an even-order permutation lies in no
  Aut(T), so it fixes no member automatically -- hence emptiness says NO
  even-order permutation stabilises any switching class at odd n.  That is
  the statement to prove.
